# Reviewer round - findings and fixes

The in-session reviewer (Claude Opus 5.5) read `git diff origin/main...HEAD`
against MO-224 and returned `VERDICT: FAIL`. Its findings, what each one
was, and the probe that shows the fix.

## 1. The night ended early but logged `ended: deadline`

**Finding (major).** `time.sleep(min(remaining, 600))` waits at most ten
minutes, so with the documented `--minutes 60` the night ended about fifty
minutes early and still logged `ended: deadline`, while `run/deadline` was
an hour later.

**Fix.** The wait is the whole window and is never cut short: `--wait`
sleeps until the window closes. The end of the night is then read from the
records, not written from a guess: the STOP file, else the clock against
`run/deadline`, else "the items were observed and the window is still
open".

**Probe.** `python3 coordinator/overnight/overnight attend --items 3
--minutes 1 --wait --run-by "the agent (rehearsal)" --root
~/Code/coordinator/.overnight/attended-2026-11-13` ran from 14:29 to
14:30 PT (the whole window), and the shift log's last line reads `ended:
deadline` with `run/deadline` behind it
(`evidence/MO-224/shift-log.md`).

## 2. The report took the shift log's word for how the night ended

**Finding (major).** `how_it_ended` returned the log's `ended:` statement
before it checked anything, so a log line `ended: all done` made the
report print "all done" with a stuck receipt, where `origin/main` printed
`UNVERIFIED`. That is the report upgrading a claim into proof.

**Fix.** The statement is now checked against the records before it is
repeated: "all done" needs every receipt to be done or parked and the
planned count to match, "deadline" needs the clock past `run/deadline`,
"STOP" needs the STOP file, "stall" needs the stall in the log. A claim
the records do not support is never repeated, and the checks that were
already there still run when the log says nothing.

**Probe.** On a folder with one stuck receipt and a deadline an hour
away, the report prints `UNVERIFIED` for a log with no statement and for a
log that says `ended: all done`, and prints `deadline` only when the
clock is past the deadline file.

## 3. The receipts claimed builder minutes that no builder used

**Finding (major).** The report's cost line read the receipt's
`Time used` as builder time, and the receipt rounded an observation up to
a minute, so the report said "Builder time: 3 min" after no builder ran.

**Fix.** The receipt records `Time used: 0 min` and puts the time the
shift spent looking in its own field, `Observed`.

**Probe.** The proof report's cost line reads "Builder time: 0 min", and
each receipt reads `- Time used: 0 min` with `- Observed: N s`
(`evidence/MO-224/MO-231.md`, `evidence/MO-224/morning-2026-11-13.md`).

## 4. The records claimed a person watched

**Finding (major).** `--attended-by Shyam` is a command-line argument, so
writing "watched by Shyam" into `run/armed` and the shift log was a claim
the command could not support. The ticket's done condition is a human
event.

**Fix.** The flag is now `--run-by`, and the records say who ran the
night. The rehearsal was run by the agent, so the records say "the agent
(rehearsal)", and this ticket states plainly that the attended hour with
Shyam has not happened yet.

**Probe.** `evidence/MO-224/shift-log.md` and `evidence/MO-224/armed` say
"run by the agent (rehearsal)".

## 5. The rule check used the shell history as proof it could not give

**Finding (major).** The rule check argued from the age of
`~/.zsh_history` that no rule was broken. A command an agent runs is not
an interactive shell command, so it never reaches the history and the
history proves nothing.

**Fix.** The check now says exactly that, and names the proof that does:
the shift log, which lists every command the night ran, and the module's
own command list, which holds no merge, no push and no force push.

**Probe.** `evidence/MO-224/rule-check.log`.

## 6. The selection had a second hold rule

**Finding (major).** The real hold is the `stage:hold` label, which the
ticket CLI keeps out of the board list. The new code instead searched
titles and problems for the word "hold", a second and parallel rule that
never sees a held ticket and wrongly skips titles that hold the word
inside another word.

**Fix.** The word search is gone. The night takes what the board shows
(`stage` is `todo`), and the board list is the only hold rule. The held
ticket FFBT-1 (#344) is not in the board list at all, and `ticket list
--fresh --json` returns 118 tickets without it.

**Probe.** `ticket list --fresh --json | python3 -c ...` shows FFBT-1 is
absent from the list, and the dry run picks MO-231, MO-234 and MO-237,
none of which is held.

## 7. The night wrote into the live receipts folder

**Finding (minor).** `attend` without `--root` armed the live runtime
folder, so the 06:30 launchd report would have mixed rehearsal receipts
into the real morning report.

**Fix.** Without `--root` the night writes to a dated folder under the
runtime folder (`attended-YYYY-MM-DD`). `--root` still points it anywhere.

**Probe.** The proof run used `--root`, and the live folder
`~/Code/coordinator/.overnight/receipts/` holds only the report from the
T12 dry run.

## 8. Smaller findings, and what was done

- The types `quick` and `light` do not exist on the board. The list is
  now the board's own small types, `chore` and `standard`.
- The gate verdict was read from comments only. It is now read from
  comments and reviews.
- The `ended:` pattern matched anywhere in a line. It now matches a line
  that ends with the statement.

## What the reviewer did not find

No path to merge, push, raise the slot cap, touch an Ascend repository,
write a secret or keep a second queue of work. The scope stayed inside
this ticket: no T4, T5, T7, T8, T9 or T10 logic was written. The fast
check passed.
